# Cloudflare Pages headers for the static site (Vite copies public/ into dist/client).
# They do not apply to Pages Functions — functions/api/_middleware.ts sets those.
#
# Framing: enforced. The depa · USDOT toolkit (depa-usdot.nonarkara.org) embeds
# /ops?view=toolkit in an iframe, so frame-ancestors allows it; nothing else may
# frame the site. (X-Frame-Options: DENY would have blocked that embed.)
#
# Content-Security-Policy: ENFORCED since 2026-10-29.
#
# It was Report-Only from 2026-10-16, with no report endpoint, so a violation
# was visible only in a console nobody read — and the note said "enforce once
# a week of use shows no violations", which is a week that never ends because
# nobody can see the violations.
#
# The question does not need a week or a collector. scripts/design/cspsweep.mjs
# loads every route at 1440 and 390 in a real browser and counts the
# securitypolicyviolation events the browser fires — the same event an
# enforced policy raises as a Report-Only one, with a different disposition.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-29 against https://bus.nonarkara.org: 24 routes × 2 widths
# = 48 pages, ZERO violations. That is the measurement the note was waiting
# for, so the policy is enforced, and the sweep now runs in the deploy path
# (cloudflare-pages.yml, and scripts/verify-deploy.sh) — an enforced policy
# that breaks a tile layer or a font fails silently, the page just looks
# wrong, and the sweep is what says it does not, on every deploy, before the
# reader finds out.
#
# connect-src, 2026-10-23: https://script.google.com and https://ipapi.co were
# REMOVED. They were there for the visitor tracker — a Google Sheet ping and an
# ipapi.co IP lookup — and the tracker is gone (src/main.tsx).
#
# What removing them actually does, stated plainly: this policy now BLOCKS
# those hosts, and the shipability audit aborts every request to them and
# fails the deploy if any route still tries. The policy and the audit are two
# different jobs — the policy is the rule, the audit is the gate — and neither
# one is the other's job.
#
# The script hash is index.html's one inline script — rebuild it if that
# script changes. A wrong hash blocks the app's own bootstrap and the page
# never starts, which the sweep sees as a violation.

